AI_SWAP4 on pcTags->origin.x modifies the buffer in place — actually mBuffer is a vector, so writing past the end of the vector is a heap-buffer-overflow WRITE. And `nd->mName.Set((const char *)pcTags->NAME)` reads.

Also note: the tag loop runs AFTER all mesh processing completes. To reach it, we need a valid MD3 file with surfaces that parse fully.

Key checks in ValidateHeaderOffsets:
- IDENT must be "IDP3"
- NUM_SURFACES != 0
- OFS_FRAMES < fileSize, OFS_SURFACES < fileSize, OFS_EOF <= fileSize
- NUM_SURFACES <= AI_MAX_ALLOC(aiMesh)
- OFS_SURFACES + NUM_SURFACES * sizeof(MD3::Surface) < fileSize
- NUM_FRAMES > configFrameID (default 0, so NUM_FRAMES >= 1)

Then surface validation: all the chunk ranges must be within file. With the integer overflow in the vulnerable version, we could exploit that, but simpler: just use the TAGS overflow since there are no checks at all on OFS_TAGS / NUM_TAGS in the vulnerable version.

Wait — ValidateHeaderOffsets doesn't check OFS_TAGS or NUM_TAGS at all, even in the header validation. The upstream added `pcTags + sizeof(MD3::Tag) * NUM_TAGS > bufferEnd` check in InternReadFile. So in the vulnerable version, setting NUM_TAGS > 0 and OFS_TAGS near end of file (or beyond) gives immediate OOB read/write.

